#{[17592186045463 #edb/ref 10 :activity.type/lesson #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045485 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/easy #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045487 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/moderate #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045493 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/radio #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045498 #edb/ref 10 :answer.type/math #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1003 #edb/ref 17592186046660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1007 "Mathematical Methods for the Physical Sciences I" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1008 "Mathematical Methods for the Physical Sciences I develops the core mathematical structures and analytical tools used throughout theoretical and applied physics. Building on calculus and introductory linear algebra, the course introduces vector geometry, multivariable analysis, and differential equations in a unified framework designed for modeling physical systems.\nStudents learn how mathematical concepts such as vector spaces, linear transformations, multivariable functions, and differential equations provide the language and computational techniques used to describe motion, fields, and dynamical processes. The course emphasizes connections between abstract mathematical ideas and the physical principles they support, preparing students for advanced study in classical mechanics, electromagnetism, and related areas of physics." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1009 "The course begins with foundational topics that support later mathematical modeling in physics. Students study vector geometry in three-dimensional space, including equations of lines and planes and geometric relationships between them. These tools appear naturally in the description of trajectories, rays, and intersections of physical surfaces. Introductory set theory provides a precise language for describing mathematical structures and regions in space, supporting later work in multivariable analysis and probability.\nStudents then develop computational tools from linear algebra. Methods such as Gaussian elimination, matrix inverses, determinants, and LU factorization are used to analyze and solve systems of linear equations. These techniques arise when solving coupled equations in physical models, such as electrical circuits, mechanical systems with multiple interacting components, and discretized approximations of physical fields.\nThe course next develops the structure of vector spaces and linear transformations. Students study spans, bases, dimension, rank, and null spaces, and extend these ideas to abstract vector spaces. Linear maps and their matrix representations are examined, establishing the connection between geometric transformations and matrix algebra. These ideas culminate in the study of eigenvalues, eigenvectors, and matrix diagonalization, which are central tools for solving differential equations describing oscillations, normal modes of vibration, and stability of dynamical systems.\nThe course then turns to multivariable calculus and vector analysis. Students analyze functions of several variables, quadric surfaces, and coordinate transformations. Partial derivatives, gradients, and the multivariable chain rule are developed along with linear approximations and higher-order derivatives. These concepts describe how physical quantities such as temperature, pressure, or electric potential vary in space. Vector-valued functions and vector fields are studied together with curvature, divergence, and curl, providing the mathematical language used to describe velocity fields in fluid flow and electric and magnetic fields in electromagnetism.\nStudents then study multiple integration, focusing on double integrals and coordinate transformations such as polar coordinates. These techniques allow the computation of physical quantities distributed over regions, such as mass, charge, or probability density.\nThe final portion of the course introduces differential equations as models of dynamical systems. Students study first-order equations and qualitative behavior of solutions through phase-line analysis. Applications include motion under drag, gravitational attraction, electrical circuits, and population dynamics. The course concludes with second-order linear differential equations and oscillatory systems, including harmonic motion, damped oscillators, and resonance—fundamental mathematical models for vibrating strings, mechanical systems, and electromagnetic circuits." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1012 154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1013 "PS1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "866ae4e9-bcd7-58f6-a7f9-92b7b1bff22c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048554 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048554 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048554 #edb/ref 1031 "Tangent Planes to Level Surfaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048554 #edb/ref 1033 1984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048554 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "a93bb5a8-5344-5f11-a0fd-45410668ecd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428471 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428472 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1031 "The Gradient as a Normal Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1033 1939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1034 0.9337212812567085 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050027 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "26b9f4f0-7964-52b3-9a23-d31ae6927cb5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128893 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3368" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128893 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+y^2+z^2,$ find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(2,-2,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128893 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "09499f38-a497-5f59-9cb2-0882a5853320" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128893 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z) = k$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(x_0,y_0,z_0) =(2,-2,1)\\mathbin{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}\\,i+{f}_{y}\\,j+{f}_{z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,-2,1) & =2(2)\\text{ }i+2(-2)\\text{ }j+2(1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\,i-4\\,j+2\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,-4\\,,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,-2,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-(-2),\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨4,\\,-4,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y+2,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 4(x-2)-4(y+2)+2(z-1) & =0 \\\\ 4x-4y+2z-18 & =0 \\\\ 2x-2y+z-9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128998 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6183" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128998 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the surface $z^3y^2 =x^2-5.$ Find the equation of the tangent plane to this surface at the point $(-1,2,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128998 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9091e2dc-5b05-5ae7-a498-9de0483ec375" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128998 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{3}{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}-5 \\\\ {z}^{3}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z) = z^3y^2 - x^2,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-5.$ \n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z) = k$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2,-1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{3}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{3}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{3}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(-2x)\\text{ }i+2{z}^{3}y\\text{ }j+3{z}^{2}{y}^{2}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(-1,2,-1) & =(-2)(-1)\\text{ }i+2(-1)^{3}(2)\\text{ }j+3(-1)^{2}(2)^{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2\\,i-4j+12\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,-4,12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,2,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),\\,y-2,\\,z-(-1)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨2,-4,12⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-2,\\,z+1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2(x+1)-4(y-2)+12(z+1) & =0 \\\\ 2x-4y+12z+22 & =0 \\\\ x-2y+6z+11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129079 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129079 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129079 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129079 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129079 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129079 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129079 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129079 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129079 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129079 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129079 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129079 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129079 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129079 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Tangent to Plane a Level Surface" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129079 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129079 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "da47f01b-0db0-586f-9d55-5bd4c952afb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129179 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129179 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129179 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129179 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129179 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129179 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129179 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129179 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129179 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186368502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129179 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186368852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129179 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129179 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129179 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129179 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Tangent Plane of a Surface Given Implicitly Using the Gradient Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129179 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129179 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "16f4bd05-1f30-576c-a538-a0ac4d533b7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129332 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129332 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129332 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129332 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129332 #edb/ref 1045 "Tangent Planes to Level Surfaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129332 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186129892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129332 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129332 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129332 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "87e23de1-746c-5124-9751-bee5befcb28f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129892 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129892 #edb/ref 1061 11201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129892 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "13f10d0e-ddd7-5833-b73f-b3f00ff7016a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129892 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129893 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129893 #edb/ref 1061 11204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129893 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6d9d3ca6-ae9b-5ac5-a35f-80fa6628ef2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129893 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130048 #edb/ref 1061 23124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130048 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f4dac68d-5f9a-5fba-b2c5-3ea5a9ec6475" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130048 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130327 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we will learn how to compute the tangent plane to the level surface $f (x, y, z) = k,$ where $k$ is a constant, at some point $\\mathbf x_0 = (x_0, y_0, z_0).$ \n\nAs an example, consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = x^2 +y^2+z.\n$$\n\n \n\nSuppose that we wish to find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=  (1,1, 2).$ \n\nEvaluating $f$ at the point $(1,1,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = (1)^2 +(1)^2+2 = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the equation of our level surface is\n\n$$\nx^2 +y^2+z = 4.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the equation of the tangent plane to this surface at $(1,1,2),$ we can use the following result:\n\n  *Given a hypersurface $w = f(x,y,z)$ and a point $\\mathbf x_0 = (x_0,y_0,z_0)$ that lies in the domain of $f$, the gradient $\\nabla \\! f (\\mathbf x_0)$ is normal to the tangent plane of the level surface $f (x, y, z) = k$ at the point $\\mathbf x_0$*. \n\n![](images/11/1140e80fbb2efd684311cb7625e5b0be361d6fb951036253f57b9e51234de3ee.png)\n\n       \n\nWe can now find the equation of the tangent plane to our level surface.\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $\\mathbf x_0 = (1,1,2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}\\,i+{f}_{y}\\,j+{f}_{z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,2) & =2(1)\\text{ }i+2(1)\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =2\\text{ }i+2\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally,  we write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle & = 0 \\[3pt]\n\\nabla f(1,1,2) \\cdot \\langle x-1, \\: y-1, \\: z-2 \\rangle & = 0 \\[3pt]\n\\langle 2,\\:2,\\:1\\rangle\\cdot \\langle x-1, y-1, z-2\\rangle &= 0\\[3pt]\n      2(x-1)+ 2(y-1) + (z-2) & =0\\\n      2x +2y +z -6&=0   \n}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130327 #edb/ref 1065 2500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130327 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7f7db883-9d6e-5a67-97d4-20274467a1e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130327 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345549 #edb/ref 1080 "x+2y+7z-36=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345549 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345549 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1663e019-c7d9-5e2d-9dbe-71ebddf8290b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345809 #edb/ref 1080 "x+8y+4z-14=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345809 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345809 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "34d6c237-b645-5134-bed0-3db87cd111a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346066 #edb/ref 1080 "x+4y+4z-10=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346066 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346066 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5451ea98-cec6-5c7d-adef-f96ea6bd234c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346236 #edb/ref 1080 "8x+y+z-23=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346236 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346236 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6571ddc7-83ee-51ae-b69b-3a73f8b9bf0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346590 #edb/ref 1080 "2x+8y+z-13=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346590 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346590 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8e60e7e0-b0eb-5453-8a9e-93ecd04c25ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346631 #edb/ref 1080 "8x+2y+5z+7=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346631 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346631 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "925d13bb-c92d-5f35-b0f3-4234c87d30e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347078 #edb/ref 1080 "x+y+z-9=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347078 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347078 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c6840705-8e54-503e-be26-3574f6f8d412" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347169 #edb/ref 1080 "x+2y+z-5=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347169 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347169 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cfc13077-10ec-5459-801a-f68bf9ba6620" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347320 #edb/ref 1080 "x+2y+5z-28=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347320 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347320 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e082281f-2341-5fb5-83fb-0ba87ed7944d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347514 #edb/ref 1080 "2x+y+z-6=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347514 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347514 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f41a2ef9-7eb7-5e8a-8edf-fd77bb60489a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347700 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186345809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347700 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347700 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347700 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186347169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347700 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186347514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347700 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347700 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347700 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186347169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347700 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "384d5f4d-e2ac-598f-9b2f-eabbb7bb39e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347954 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186345549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347954 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347954 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347954 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186347078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347954 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186347320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347954 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347954 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347954 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186347320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347954 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c9a7661b-5846-57e9-be3c-6d40db58cd30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348448 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348448 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80767" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348448 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348448 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=xy-yz-xz$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes  through the point where $(x,y,z)=(2,3,4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348448 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bbcded61-18ec-5362-9f48-c67e58a60e0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348448 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(2,3,4):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-yz-xz)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-yz-xz)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy-yz-xz)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(y-z)\\text{ }i+(x-z)\\text{ }j+(-y-x)k \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,3,4) & =(3-4)\\text{ }i+(2-4)\\text{ }j+(-3-2)k \\\\  & =-1\\text{ }i-2\\text{ }j-5k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(2,3,4)\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-3,\\,z-4⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-1,-2,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-3,\\,z-4⟩ & =0 \\\\ -(x-2)-2(y-3)-5(z-4) & =0 \\\\ -x-2y-5z+28 & =0 \\\\ x+2y+5z-28 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348449 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348449 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80769" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348449 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348449 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}$,  find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(2,1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348449 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4798bf87-b861-5528-ad8e-12b959ab1ff8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348449 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(2,1,1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+8y\\text{ }j+4z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,1,1) & =2(2)\\text{ }i+8(1)j+4(1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\,i+8\\,j+4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨4,8,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-1,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨4,8,4⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-1,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 4(x-2)+8(y-1)+4(z-1) & =0 \\\\ 4x+8y+4z-20 & =0 \\\\ x+2y+z-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368502 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186368503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368502 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368502 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368502 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the surface $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+2{z}^{2})=1-x.$ Find the equation of the tangent plane to that surface at the point $(1,-2,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368502 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2fd20ce6-a0d3-5928-9428-ede66532d00e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368502 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+2{z}^{2}) & =1-x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+2{z}^{2})+x-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+2{z}^{2})+x-1$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,-2,1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+2{z}^{2})+x-1\\,)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+2{z}^{2})+x-1\\,)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+2{z}^{2})+x-1\\,)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\text{ }i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+2{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+4z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+2{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,-2,1) & =\\text{ }i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-2)+2(1)^{2})\\text{ }j+4(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-2)+2(1)^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =i+j+4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+2,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨1,1,4⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+2,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ (x-1)+(y+2)+4(z-1) & =0 \\\\ x+y+4z-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] 